[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-99113"] :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+3y-z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=11.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=11.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=11\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3=2\\lambda y \\\\ -1=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=11$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{11}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =11 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=\\pm 3,\\,z=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(1,3,-1),\\,(-1,-3,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,3,-1)$ | $(-1,-3,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $11$ | $-11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,-3,1),$ and its minimum value is $-11.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-101310/selection/60d8696f019bd6901d4028773e8b64c56a3047a6bc6b4ed12b314cf6b1ae175a" :answer/id #uuid "3b9cafcb-58df-576b-9c08-131583771c13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101310/selection/008b962e8dba6a793a19582d7b90d25ecc915fb39cdb8821fa797599cabe57ab" :answer/id #uuid "2f41d8e0-9dbc-5418-ba0e-c763a8290ecb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101310/selection/aa57a8d17f569b619e62bd602a6af7cff5d106888256da51f23b5b66e6987fe8" :answer/id #uuid "12779d99-4202-566d-9cc0-4854cc58759c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-26" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101310/selection/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" :answer/id #uuid "f5972f38-6314-5b9b-adb2-a2de311a9b84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101310/selection/ac46572169135c39bc2dc1bfb75349ed12f60a1187a3ef59cacec084b49d12ff" :answer/id #uuid "6bb80e85-bf93-5673-b51e-cc95c396e584" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101310/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "52cefdc7-90a7-5c36-8f10-002a6cd113cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-101310/selection/ac46572169135c39bc2dc1bfb75349ed12f60a1187a3ef59cacec084b49d12ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-101310/selection/60d8696f019bd6901d4028773e8b64c56a3047a6bc6b4ed12b314cf6b1ae175a" "q-101310/selection/008b962e8dba6a793a19582d7b90d25ecc915fb39cdb8821fa797599cabe57ab" "q-101310/selection/aa57a8d17f569b619e62bd602a6af7cff5d106888256da51f23b5b66e6987fe8" "q-101310/selection/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" "q-101310/selection/ac46572169135c39bc2dc1bfb75349ed12f60a1187a3ef59cacec084b49d12ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-101310" :question/id #uuid "83b46289-006f-5fe0-b0f6-c2019e283781" :question/math-academy-id "q-101310" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=3-y+5z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=26.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=26.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=26\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,-1,5⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y \\\\ 5=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=26$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =26 \\\\ 0+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =26 \\\\ \\frac{26}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =26 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=∓1\\,z=\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(0,-1,5),\\,(0,1,-5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-1,5)\\,$ | $\\,(0,1,-5)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,29\\,$ | $\\,-23\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,1,-5),$ and its minimum value is $-23.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-101310/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-101310"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162763/selection/10293ac0295e0e79554137a949f5c5c46b9812dcc0a3ecb6349c4cb5e6acdff3" :answer/id #uuid "3f3be6cf-3903-5378-bb81-464589a38db0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162763/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "e567881c-5b9a-5ba4-ab90-64132c4e7937" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162763/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "4a882165-d91f-5287-be3a-e998ec0dd085" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162763/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "8f00bcb0-d0c9-5b7b-a15d-3ab9ef86bf8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162763/selection/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" :answer/id #uuid "6bc291d4-5f9e-538d-93b3-e6fc732457cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162763/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a717293-313c-513f-b79d-334f7ad34d9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162763/selection/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" :answer-field/choices ["q-162763/selection/10293ac0295e0e79554137a949f5c5c46b9812dcc0a3ecb6349c4cb5e6acdff3" "q-162763/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-162763/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-162763/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-162763/selection/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162763" :question/id #uuid "e912a000-72e3-5446-81b8-633418a71a74" :question/math-academy-id "q-162763" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4y-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,4-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 4-2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2)=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\sqrt{5}),(0,\\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-2+1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(1,-2),(-1,-2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(\\pm 1,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-\\sqrt{5})$ | $(0,\\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 1,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $-14$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 1,-2)$ and its minimum value is $-14.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162763/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162763"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162764/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "db8fb7b9-9f70-5adb-bffa-2e4d7e2e5dd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162764/selection/10293ac0295e0e79554137a949f5c5c46b9812dcc0a3ecb6349c4cb5e6acdff3" :answer/id #uuid "bcc5194d-0338-5f62-b5b1-afc0a859588e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162764/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "e9ee8750-38ff-5699-b7eb-991e293c8ef5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162764/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a7c8970a-8e3a-5887-96da-508eb8ffb593" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162764/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "623ee345-c260-5f33-9eec-620ffb1fa88e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162764/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6173ad26-93ee-533b-9fca-b7a634522cf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162764/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-162764/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" "q-162764/selection/10293ac0295e0e79554137a949f5c5c46b9812dcc0a3ecb6349c4cb5e6acdff3" "q-162764/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-162764/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-162764/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162764" :question/id #uuid "e18d11ea-8484-5205-94a7-e010311a7706" :question/math-academy-id "q-162764" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-2),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2+1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(2,0),(-2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-3$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 2)$ and its minimum value is $-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162764/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162764"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162784/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "08fa10f5-b10f-5f7a-a387-d7d0dc973c8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162784/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "bf238f05-8958-5ebf-89a3-366936367997" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162784/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "cc9bcab5-d4a7-596b-8317-2c0c8b0a31c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162784/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "ce3a770e-5238-53d0-8247-c44e450fdc16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162784/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "94d422df-b70c-5733-b2bb-5074e71b918b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162784/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "aeba2af3-e438-55ff-b552-30b57c0ac261" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162784/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-162784/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-162784/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" "q-162784/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-162784/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-162784/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162784" :question/id #uuid "d01be1ef-86d9-5ac6-9c63-a5883e47e009" :question/math-academy-id "q-162784" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3$ on the surface ${z}^{2}-xy-4=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y,z)$ exists on the surface.*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-xy-4=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-y,-x,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x=-\\lambda y \\\\ 2y=-\\lambda x \\\\ 2z=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}(4-{\\lambda }^{2})x=0 \\\\ (4-{\\lambda }^{2})y=0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm 2.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =-2.$ Then we must have $x=y$ and $z=0.$ But this also contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Now, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=y=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-4=0\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =2.$ Then we must have $x=-y$ and $z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-(-y)y-4=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(∓2,\\pm 2,0).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,\\pm 2)\\,$ | $\\,(∓2,\\pm 2,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,11\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,0,\\pm 2),$ and its minimum value is $7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-162784/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162784"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
